Before you start
1.	VS Code 1.85 or newer. Check this under Help → About.
2.	Python 3.9 or newer. Open a terminal and run python --version (on Mac or Linux, python3 --version). If it isn't installed, get it from python.org. On Windows, tick "Add Python to PATH" during setup.

Install the extension
1.	Download python-oop-visualizer-0.3.0.vsix from the file card above. It's the .vsix file, not the zip.
2.	In VS Code, open the Extensions view (Ctrl+Shift+X, or Cmd+Shift+X on Mac).
3.	Click the … menu at the top right of that panel and choose Install from VSIX….
4.	Pick the downloaded .vsix file. Restart VS Code if it asks.(Press => Ctrl+Shift+P and then select Developer: Reload Window )

Alternatively, run this in a terminal from the folder containing the file(NOTE:please place the downloaded filei.e."python-oop-visualizer-0.3.0.vsix" inside the project folder before executing the terminal command):
code --install-extension python-oop-visualizer-0.3.0.vsix

If you installed 0.2.0 before, uninstall it first (Extensions → "Python OOP Visualizer" → Uninstall).
Check that it works
1.	Open any .py file. You can use examples/01_student.py from the zip.
2.	Press Ctrl+Alt+V (Mac: Cmd+Alt+V).
3.	A visualizer window should open. Drag it next to VS Code or onto a second screen. You should see the Program output (live) box, then the class diagram.
To open the lessons, press Ctrl+Shift+P and run OOP Visualizer: Open Lessons (Learn).

